#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124333 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124524 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d443b8e-3e50-53a7-b3a5-a9d1b85b5f19" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of a One-Dimensional Eigenspace"}] :question/id #uuid "a1068af3-0424-51ae-8567-7e1503209d2e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5493" :question/problem "![](images/36/36280c9a2392e04a68646d40908f6041a28017e63d6f8d96b25b518428cb9717.png)\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a basis of the eigenspace corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-4.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-4.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5{x}_{2}+8{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $2$nd and the $3$rd). Thus, $x_1$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain $x_3=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get $x_2=0.$ \n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n![](images/43/4366abc9a1d46153708ef49ca06b25406739e06c5eaf3ee8c1a10c80081590f8.png)\n\nTherefore, the required basis is\n![](images/32/32cc18b0a63d50f6c20119cf7a437d7998123770320dcbfbe7e84f4833b2ff19.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124334 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124525 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d945f26-273d-5648-bdee-9092ea61110b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of a Two-Dimensional Eigenspace"}] :question/id #uuid "e82a8038-a2fa-547d-b3ec-32e8f0e4babd" :question/math-academy-id "e-5494" :question/problem "![](images/b0/b065c3d310f87a61b96a72df966699f7f8497213a47e26d91b4ae93fc8b59c70.png)\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a basis of the eigenspace corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 8 & -6 \\\\ -15 & 14 & -9 \\\\ -5 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 8 & -6 \\\\ -15 & 12 & -9 \\\\ -5 & 4 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-10{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -15{x}_{1}+12{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 8 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -15 & 12 & -9 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 4 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -15 & 12 & -9 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 4 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ and $x_3$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=\\dfrac 4 5x_2-\\dfrac35x_3.$ So, the general solution is\n\n![](images/55/553b7f6a207b31771ec5666fabd9a1c3d0d94aa690a7679816098836d383ac36.png)\n\nTherefore, the required basis is \n![](images/e2/e26193e087780f09c7066f9f4057adc7fc3711e347c9be2a2dfbb9080595c4a9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124335 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124526 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0631858c-a25d-564a-8b0f-b21506d844a2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Dimension of an Eigenspace"}] :question/id #uuid "7f15ec22-8a8e-5547-9aa1-7ffa8b1555a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-5495" :question/problem "![](images/44/442662066e9f90922c5113cad57faf1d735471da422963f8c19a7e09e6798c41.png)\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. What are the dimensions of the eigenspaces $V_{0}$ and $V_{-2}$ corresponding to eigenvalues $\\lambda=0$ and $\\lambda=-2$ of $A$, respectively?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the characteristic equation $\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ 1 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )+3)=0 \\\\ -(\\lambda +2)({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda )=0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda +2)^{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda_1=0$ (simple root) and $\\lambda_2=\\lambda_3=-2$ (double root). \n\nLet's now consider the eigenspaces corresponding to each eigenvector:\n\n- First, we consider $\\lambda=0.$ Since $V_{0}$ corresponds to a simple root, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(V_{0})=1.\n$$\n\n- Then, we consider $\\lambda=-2,$ which is a repeated root of the characteristic equation. To find the dimension of $V_{-2},$ we row-reduce the matrix $(A - (-2) I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). This implies that we will have $3-1=2$ linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = -2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(V_{-2})=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124336 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124527 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e7473be-4d68-5d31-817f-adb7cf288e97" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Linearly Independent Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "483a0b64-adfa-50b7-a738-2cdc135b1163" :question/math-academy-id "e-5496" :question/problem "![](images/54/54485bcac53abb10709e40252f01b39eb5769c382f3c3fd0c2434bd0bdeb2548.png)\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2$ shown above.\nIf $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are two linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4,$ what is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =4.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -8 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -12 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}+10{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -12 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ and $x_3$ are free variables. On the other hand, from the first equation, we obtain $x_1=2x_2-\\dfrac{5}{3}x_3.$ \n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n![](images/f4/f45f16c7521123658c524c4163e153716ab21f2b85adaef3e5a10a9e920b5cc7.png)\n\nWe now deduce the eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2\\mathbin :$\n\n- Since ![](images/5d/5d8b49b079891f5644f62ae6f39f51f6714a553366468f44de48074e26ce7729.png) we must have that $x_2=1$ and $x_3=0.$ As a result, $a=2(1)-\\dfrac 53(0)=2.$\n\n- Since ![](images/00/00357c5709df9b476f1f9bb86bb043d15c441a480cc7e882b797454b74ac2f1d.png) we must have that $x_2=0$ and $x_3=3.$ As a result, $b=2(0)-\\dfrac53(3)=-5.$\n\nTherefore, $a+b=2+(-5)=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272152 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124525 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d945f26-273d-5648-bdee-9092ea61110b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of a Two-Dimensional Eigenspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9462f65e-eea2-5e80-9bf8-00c46888bb01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270567} {:answer/id #uuid "9998a015-82d2-5e59-88a2-dd42cefbc793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270619} {:answer/id #uuid "9f6c7789-ed20-551d-8235-d5314d57d582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270686} {:answer/id #uuid "a3439cc9-cdc0-5750-bb4c-227de586a297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270722} {:answer/id #uuid "bb5a8379-f0c4-5fd1-a69d-4b38165f0f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9998a015-82d2-5e59-88a2-dd42cefbc793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270619} :answer-field/id #uuid "74eb201d-1823-5f28-80d6-afd14c8c4ffa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6eca6595-d579-5346-8866-6734afec24b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-140750" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Which of the following is a basis of the eigenspace corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required basis is\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125025 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124526 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0631858c-a25d-564a-8b0f-b21506d844a2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Dimension of an Eigenspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19e1b3ae-9168-5b02-910e-2dc54a001c76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{dim}({V}_{-6})=2$ and $\\text{dim}({V}_{2})=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321347} {:answer/id #uuid "71c90065-83a9-5737-948d-9f726b991416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{dim}({V}_{-6})=2$ and $\\text{dim}({V}_{2})=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322041} {:answer/id #uuid "a81c8699-2b1b-5e76-998e-729850bba051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{dim}({V}_{-6})=1$ and $\\text{dim}({V}_{2})=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322462} {:answer/id #uuid "df0d58c4-eac7-5499-8687-b8ce0a50c1ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{dim}({V}_{-6})=1$ and $\\text{dim}({V}_{2})=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322892} {:answer/id #uuid "f26b3abc-dd8e-5d54-9a19-e61fb814fac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{dim}({V}_{-6})=0$ and $\\text{dim}({V}_{2})=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df0d58c4-eac7-5499-8687-b8ce0a50c1ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{dim}({V}_{-6})=1$ and $\\text{dim}({V}_{2})=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322892} :answer-field/id #uuid "a271b6a7-47ed-514f-9610-bf67a8612896" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323485}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b330ccf8-bb5f-5d71-ad55-322ed64e004b" :question/math-academy-id "q-36981" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. What are the dimensions of the eigenspaces ${V}_{-6}$ and ${V}_{2}$ corresponding to eigenvalues $\\lambda =-6$ and $\\lambda =2$ of $A$, respectively?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-8-\\lambda  & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-8-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ -5 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((-8-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )+20)=0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda -12)=0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(\\lambda +6)(\\lambda -2)=0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2}(\\lambda +6)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=2$ (double root) and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-6$ (simple root).\n\nLet's now consider the eigenspaces corresponding to each eigenvector:\n\n- First, we consider $\\lambda =-6.$ Since ${V}_{-6}$ corresponds to a simple root, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{-6})=1.\n$$\n\n- Then, we consider $\\lambda =2,$ which is a repeated root of the characteristic equation. To find the dimension of ${V}_{2},$ we row-reduce the matrix $(A-2I)\\text{x}=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-10 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). This implies that we will have $3-1=2$ linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{2})=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124524 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d443b8e-3e50-53a7-b3a5-a9d1b85b5f19" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of a One-Dimensional Eigenspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80954a0e-6937-5e55-ae5a-8c6449fee3b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392047} {:answer/id #uuid "2905f882-f7c6-578b-9335-7944d5155574" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392048} {:answer/id #uuid "67b48ad4-df5f-58a2-863e-2050d42189bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392049} {:answer/id #uuid "fc38e808-1e49-5191-9975-06aeef2129e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392050} {:answer/id #uuid "b4a1334d-4cf9-5c4d-9987-d3077a269a19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4a1334d-4cf9-5c4d-9987-d3077a269a19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392051} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5249c62-4514-561e-a0aa-bc74ec85e1db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54903b20-c858-5f32-b074-5d4ac7d7a586" :question/math-academy-id "q-36982" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Which of the following is a basis of the eigenspace corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 13 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=0.$ \n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required basis is\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124526 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0631858c-a25d-564a-8b0f-b21506d844a2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Dimension of an Eigenspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95325e4e-fa94-5972-be61-5789c3bd7fb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "dim(V_{5}) = 1, dim(V_{-2}) = 1, dim(V_{1}) = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123021} {:answer/id #uuid "b4524de2-5bb6-505d-9bb8-0d744d49dec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "dim(V_{5}) = 1, dim(V_{-2}) = 1, dim(V_{1}) = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123022} {:answer/id #uuid "b68bf487-85c1-58a6-b563-46a7bdfaf398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "dim(V_{5}) = 2, dim(V_{-2}) = 2, dim(V_{1}) = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123023} {:answer/id #uuid "5b54ae4a-f65a-5ab5-8fa3-a81ba1895445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "dim(V_{5}) = 1, dim(V_{-2}) = 2, dim(V_{1}) = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123024} {:answer/id #uuid "69946f6f-11d0-572e-a545-9bd6190bfe9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "dim(V_{5}) = 1, dim(V_{-2}) = 1, dim(V_{1}) = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123025}] :answer-field/id #uuid "52e546ee-92c8-59a0-89c6-8d948fa6d74d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123020}] :question/id #uuid "de041a54-b101-57bd-a92d-71e0c07a0aca" :question/math-academy-id "q-37407" :question/problem "$C = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $C$ shown above. What are the dimensions of the eigenspaces $V_{5}$, $V_{-2}$, and $V_{1}$ corresponding to eigenvalues $λ = 5$, $λ =-2$, and $λ = 1$ of $C$, respectively?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323920 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124524 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d443b8e-3e50-53a7-b3a5-a9d1b85b5f19" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of a One-Dimensional Eigenspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01fe15f2-14d9-5b88-8a54-f65fb181ae73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321166} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7c637b-ee72-51f9-a474-a608289366c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322149} {:answer/id #uuid "a900a1b7-ee77-5c84-8391-6bc85e7201a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322475} {:answer/id #uuid "d9d6eea0-e669-5188-a8a7-027513f1defc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322853} {:answer/id #uuid "e6ba3be6-a2e7-54e8-a20e-2eaefe13e163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f7c637b-ee72-51f9-a474-a608289366c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322149} :answer-field/id #uuid "f01bdb43-f090-535a-83e6-4e6e2bd22c39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ed416fd-31fc-5c4a-aaad-80e5dc0cc476" :question/math-academy-id "q-37577" :question/problem "$$\nG=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $G$ shown above. Which of the following is a basis of the eigenspace corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(G-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that its augmented matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $2$nd and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{2}=0.$ \n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required basis is \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125079 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124524 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d443b8e-3e50-53a7-b3a5-a9d1b85b5f19" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of a One-Dimensional Eigenspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices 